  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$ \n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty $",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14257461/assets/ee12a13d8f5faf2fe3883487bfc212e173d8b364d18a4b540a4b4394f5102ae1.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, it's clear that the function is increasing.\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ increases, $y$ increases without bound.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not defined for $x\\le 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true.",
